1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,640
$6.3 billion.

2
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:02,440
Gone,

3
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,520
in about 20 minutes.

4
00:00:04,053 --> 00:00:05,713
This is the biggest crypto theft

5
00:00:05,513 --> 00:00:06,253
in history.

6
00:00:06,813 --> 00:00:08,313
Here's exactly how it happened,

7
00:00:08,593 --> 00:00:09,233
who did it,

8
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:10,693
and what it means for anyone

9
00:00:10,493 --> 00:00:12,013
who keeps money on an exchange.

10
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:14,130
First,

11
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:15,090
how big is this?

12
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:17,170
Here are the biggest crypto thefts

13
00:00:16,970 --> 00:00:17,510
ever.

14
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:18,230
Mt.

15
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:19,270
Gox in 2014,

16
00:00:19,070 --> 00:00:20,970
about 460 million dollars.

17
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:23,130
Poly Network and Ronin,

18
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:24,870
a little over 600 million each.

19
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:26,550
Bybit in 2025,

20
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:28,090
about 1.5 billion.

21
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:29,687
And Binance:

22
00:00:29,487 --> 00:00:31,487
about $6.3 billion.

23
00:00:32,431 --> 00:00:34,391
That's roughly four times the old

24
00:00:34,191 --> 00:00:34,711
record.

25
00:00:35,337 --> 00:00:36,957
It's even more than North Korea

26
00:00:36,757 --> 00:00:38,577
stole in all of 2025,

27
00:00:39,197 --> 00:00:40,897
which was already a record at

28
00:00:40,697 --> 00:00:43,077
about 2.02 billion dollars.

29
00:00:44,517 --> 00:00:45,997
To understand how it happened,

30
00:00:46,317 --> 00:00:47,297
you need to know where an

31
00:00:47,097 --> 00:00:48,357
exchange keeps your crypto.

32
00:00:48,977 --> 00:00:50,017
When you buy on an exchange,

33
00:00:50,217 --> 00:00:51,617
the coins usually sit in wallets

34
00:00:51,417 --> 00:00:52,497
the exchange controls,

35
00:00:52,817 --> 00:00:53,417
not you.

36
00:00:54,125 --> 00:00:55,325
Most of it is kept in

37
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:55,925
cold storage:

38
00:00:56,065 --> 00:00:56,545
offline,

39
00:00:56,765 --> 00:00:57,665
much harder to reach.

40
00:00:58,428 --> 00:00:59,788
But some has to stay online,

41
00:01:00,068 --> 00:01:01,268
in so-called hot wallets,

42
00:01:01,428 --> 00:01:02,748
so the exchange can pay out

43
00:01:02,548 --> 00:01:03,388
withdrawals quickly.

44
00:01:04,121 --> 00:01:04,761
This time,

45
00:01:04,821 --> 00:01:06,101
it was the hot wallets that

46
00:01:05,901 --> 00:01:06,441
were drained.

47
00:01:06,921 --> 00:01:08,301
That's where the attackers aimed.

48
00:01:09,717 --> 00:01:10,957
So how did they get in?

49
00:01:11,477 --> 00:01:13,337
Binance's first findings point to one

50
00:01:13,137 --> 00:01:14,137
engineer's laptop.

51
00:01:14,817 --> 00:01:15,937
Hackers compromised it,

52
00:01:15,737 --> 00:01:17,117
and that gave them access to

53
00:01:16,917 --> 00:01:17,837
a service container,

54
00:01:18,137 --> 00:01:19,557
a piece of the exchange's internal

55
00:01:19,357 --> 00:01:20,017
systems.

56
00:01:20,844 --> 00:01:22,424
Binance says it has found no

57
00:01:22,224 --> 00:01:23,784
evidence that its private keys,

58
00:01:23,904 --> 00:01:25,584
the master passwords to its wallets,

59
00:01:25,784 --> 00:01:26,204
leaked.

60
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:28,510
But Binance's co-CEO,

61
00:01:28,790 --> 00:01:29,890
Richard Teng,

62
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:31,090
has said the attack was more

63
00:01:30,890 --> 00:01:32,650
complex than one engineer's access.

64
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:34,690
The full incident report is still

65
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:35,050
to come.

66
00:01:36,517 --> 00:01:37,777
What we do know is that

67
00:01:37,577 --> 00:01:38,317
this was planned.

68
00:01:38,997 --> 00:01:41,157
Blockchain investigators at TRM Labs found

69
00:01:40,957 --> 00:01:42,697
the staging started well before the

70
00:01:42,497 --> 00:01:42,897
theft.

71
00:01:43,509 --> 00:01:45,109
Then it all happened at once.

72
00:01:45,409 --> 00:01:46,329
In TRM's words:

73
00:01:46,469 --> 00:01:47,869
signatures obtained in advance,

74
00:01:47,669 --> 00:01:49,669
then executed in one burst.

75
00:01:50,292 --> 00:01:51,852
The money moved across about 10

76
00:01:51,652 --> 00:01:52,552
different blockchains,

77
00:01:52,572 --> 00:01:53,552
and most of it was gone

78
00:01:53,352 --> 00:01:54,512
in about 20 minutes.

79
00:01:55,883 --> 00:01:57,543
Step two for any thief is

80
00:01:57,343 --> 00:01:58,163
hiding the money.

81
00:01:58,583 --> 00:02:00,343
The stolen coins included all kinds

82
00:02:00,143 --> 00:02:00,723
of tokens.

83
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:02,850
According to the FBI,

84
00:02:03,230 --> 00:02:04,590
the hackers have been converting them

85
00:02:04,390 --> 00:02:05,490
mostly into Ethereum,

86
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:07,610
and it expects more laundering steps

87
00:02:07,410 --> 00:02:08,110
to follow.

88
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:10,060
Here's why that matters.

89
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:11,920
Some crypto can be frozen.

90
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:14,880
Stablecoins like Tether and USDC are

91
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:16,180
run by companies that can block

92
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:16,860
stolen coins.

93
00:02:17,737 --> 00:02:19,997
Bitcoin and Ethereum have no company

94
00:02:19,797 --> 00:02:20,517
in charge.

95
00:02:20,777 --> 00:02:21,977
No one can freeze them.

96
00:02:22,511 --> 00:02:23,211
So far,

97
00:02:23,231 --> 00:02:25,411
Binance says about $3 billion has been

98
00:02:25,211 --> 00:02:27,151
frozen with help from partners including

99
00:02:26,951 --> 00:02:28,131
Tether and Circle.

100
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:30,330
Who did it?

101
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:32,350
On October 31st,

102
00:02:32,290 --> 00:02:34,150
the FBI said North Korea was

103
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:34,550
responsible,

104
00:02:34,910 --> 00:02:36,570
naming a hacking group known as

105
00:02:36,370 --> 00:02:37,826
TraderTraitor.

106
00:02:38,026 --> 00:02:40,306
Investigators say the attack follows the

107
00:02:40,106 --> 00:02:41,786
same playbook as the Bybit hack

108
00:02:41,586 --> 00:02:44,446
in 2025 and the KelpDAO hack

109
00:02:44,246 --> 00:02:45,086
this April,

110
00:02:44,886 --> 00:02:46,706
right down to how the attackers

111
00:02:46,506 --> 00:02:47,606
funded their wallets.

112
00:02:48,458 --> 00:02:49,518
And this is a pattern.

113
00:02:50,198 --> 00:02:51,718
According to Chainalysis,

114
00:02:51,518 --> 00:02:54,118
North Korea-linked hackers stole about 2.02

115
00:02:53,918 --> 00:02:56,338
billion dollars in crypto in 2025,

116
00:02:56,138 --> 00:02:57,098
a record.

117
00:02:57,932 --> 00:02:59,872
That was about 59% of all

118
00:02:59,672 --> 00:03:01,612
the crypto stolen worldwide that year.

119
00:03:02,107 --> 00:03:04,347
United Nations experts have said this

120
00:03:04,147 --> 00:03:06,127
stolen money helps fund the country's

121
00:03:05,927 --> 00:03:06,787
weapons programs.

122
00:03:08,350 --> 00:03:09,270
So who pays?

123
00:03:09,790 --> 00:03:11,310
Binance says it will absorb the

124
00:03:11,110 --> 00:03:11,730
losses,

125
00:03:11,890 --> 00:03:12,670
not its customers.

126
00:03:13,606 --> 00:03:15,486
It's using its emergency fund for

127
00:03:15,286 --> 00:03:15,786
users,

128
00:03:16,206 --> 00:03:17,406
called SAFU,

129
00:03:17,586 --> 00:03:19,286
part of its futures insurance fund,

130
00:03:19,486 --> 00:03:20,906
and money from its own treasury.

131
00:03:21,689 --> 00:03:22,549
On Saturday,

132
00:03:22,709 --> 00:03:24,749
Binance said about 90% of the

133
00:03:24,549 --> 00:03:26,029
affected assets were covered.

134
00:03:26,631 --> 00:03:27,171
By Sunday,

135
00:03:27,291 --> 00:03:28,871
it said 95%.

136
00:03:29,484 --> 00:03:30,624
The 10 biggest coins,

137
00:03:30,644 --> 00:03:32,024
including bitcoin and ether,

138
00:03:32,084 --> 00:03:32,844
are fully backed,

139
00:03:32,924 --> 00:03:33,384
it says.

140
00:03:33,904 --> 00:03:35,684
The rest are smaller tokens it's

141
00:03:35,484 --> 00:03:36,304
still buying back.

142
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:38,850
Right after the hack,

143
00:03:39,170 --> 00:03:41,310
Binance paused deposits and withdrawals.

144
00:03:42,178 --> 00:03:42,978
On Sunday,

145
00:03:42,918 --> 00:03:43,998
November 1st,

146
00:03:44,158 --> 00:03:45,978
withdrawals started coming back in phases:

147
00:03:46,618 --> 00:03:47,718
first the Bitcoin network,

148
00:03:48,178 --> 00:03:50,378
then Ethereum and BNB Smart Chain.

149
00:03:51,048 --> 00:03:52,248
Binance warned of delays,

150
00:03:52,048 --> 00:03:53,888
and says a fuller incident report

151
00:03:53,688 --> 00:03:55,568
will follow once its investigation is

152
00:03:55,368 --> 00:03:55,808
done.

153
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:58,570
The news hit the whole market.

154
00:03:58,910 --> 00:03:59,730
The night of the hack,

155
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:02,370
bitcoin briefly fell below $77,000.

156
00:04:03,238 --> 00:04:04,118
It was a scare,

157
00:04:04,378 --> 00:04:05,078
not a collapse,

158
00:04:05,558 --> 00:04:07,138
but it shows how much of

159
00:04:06,938 --> 00:04:09,158
crypto runs through a single exchange.

160
00:04:10,717 --> 00:04:12,317
So what does this mean for

161
00:04:12,117 --> 00:04:12,517
you?

162
00:04:12,677 --> 00:04:14,017
We don't give financial advice,

163
00:04:14,337 --> 00:04:15,977
but this hack teaches four things.

164
00:04:16,672 --> 00:04:17,252
One:

165
00:04:17,432 --> 00:04:18,812
when your crypto sits on an

166
00:04:18,612 --> 00:04:18,992
exchange,

167
00:04:19,672 --> 00:04:20,872
the exchange holds the keys.

168
00:04:21,552 --> 00:04:22,652
You're trusting its security.

169
00:04:23,455 --> 00:04:23,935
Two:

170
00:04:23,735 --> 00:04:25,975
an exchange's reserves and insurance funds

171
00:04:25,775 --> 00:04:26,395
matter.

172
00:04:26,695 --> 00:04:28,315
They're why Binance customers may not

173
00:04:28,115 --> 00:04:29,175
lose money this time.

174
00:04:29,756 --> 00:04:30,236
Three:

175
00:04:30,416 --> 00:04:31,596
after every big hack,

176
00:04:31,756 --> 00:04:33,596
scammers pretend to be support staff.

177
00:04:33,856 --> 00:04:35,456
Real support will never ask for

178
00:04:35,256 --> 00:04:37,196
your password or your recovery phrase.

179
00:04:37,797 --> 00:04:38,217
Four:

180
00:04:38,497 --> 00:04:40,097
holding crypto yourself removes the exchange

181
00:04:39,897 --> 00:04:40,597
risk,

182
00:04:40,697 --> 00:04:41,837
but if you lose your own

183
00:04:41,637 --> 00:04:42,077
keys,

184
00:04:42,257 --> 00:04:43,297
nobody can get them back.

185
00:04:44,750 --> 00:04:45,570
Let's recap.

186
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:47,430
About $6.3 billion,

187
00:04:47,910 --> 00:04:49,690
the biggest crypto theft ever,

188
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:51,510
taken in one planned burst of

189
00:04:51,310 --> 00:04:52,270
about 20 minutes.

190
00:04:52,770 --> 00:04:54,490
The FBI says North Korea did

191
00:04:54,290 --> 00:04:54,670
it.

192
00:04:54,850 --> 00:04:56,270
And Binance says it has covered

193
00:04:56,070 --> 00:04:57,730
95% so far,

194
00:04:57,530 --> 00:04:58,650
with its own money.

195
00:04:59,126 --> 00:05:00,386
Would you keep your crypto on

196
00:05:00,186 --> 00:05:01,346
an exchange after this?

197
00:05:01,566 --> 00:05:02,506
Tell me in the comments,

198
00:05:02,306 --> 00:05:04,006
and subscribe so you don't miss

199
00:05:03,806 --> 00:05:05,266
the full incident report when it

200
00:05:05,066 --> 00:05:05,686
comes out.

201
00:05:06,284 --> 00:05:08,384
This video is for education only.

202
00:05:08,844 --> 00:05:10,144
It's not financial advice.

